<h1>TLiteral</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TLiteral" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TLiteral</tt> displays static text on a Web page. It resembles <a href="?page=Controls.Label">TLabel</a>, except that <tt>TLiteral</tt> extends <tt>TControl</tt> rather than <tt>TWebControl</tt>. It therefore has no style properties such as <tt>BackColor</tt> or <tt>Font</tt>, and it renders no element of its own. The text appears exactly where the control sits.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The text displayed by <tt>TLiteral</tt> can be programmatically controlled by setting the <tt>Text</tt> property. The text displayed may be HTML-encoded if the <tt>Encode</tt> is true (the default value is false).
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TLiteral</tt> will render the contents enclosed within its component tag if <tt>Text</tt> is empty.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Be aware, if <tt>Encode</tt> is false, make sure <tt>Text</tt> does not contain unwanted characters that may bring security vulnerabilities.
</p>

<h2>Encoding</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Encode</tt> decides how the text reaches the page.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Value</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>false</td>
	<td>The text renders as written, markup included. This is the default.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>true</td>
	<td>The control HTML-encodes the text, so markup shows as characters rather than acting as markup.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Text that comes from a user belongs in a literal with <tt>Encode</tt> set to true.
<tt>Data</tt> is another name for <tt>Text</tt>, which lets a literal serve as the data
renderer of a templated control.
</p>

